#  Lessons learned deploying this

Getting this from code to a working, verified pipeline surfaced a couple of
real infrastructure problems worth documenting:

- Git history and large binaries — my first commit accidentally included
  the `.terraform/` provider cache, which bundled a ~140 MB AWS provider
  binary. This caused repeated push timeouts to GitHub. Fixed by adding a
  proper `.gitignore` (`.terraform/`, `*.tfstate`, etc.) and rebuilding the
  commit history so the binary was never tracked, rather than trying to
  scrub it after the fact.
  
- CI credentials — the GitHub Actions runner has no AWS credentials by
  default, so `terraform plan` failed in CI with "No valid credential
  sources found" even though it worked fine locally (where the AWS CLI was
  already configured). Fixed by storing the access key as encrypted GitHub
  Secrets and mapping them to `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` / `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`
  environment variables in the workflow, rather than hardcoding anything.

Both are common real-world gaps between "works on my machine" and "works in
CI" — good reminders that a policy-as-code pipeline needs its own working
credentials and clean git hygiene, not just correct Terraform.